  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Pilippines PMU Staff</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam defines a Just Energy Transition (JET) based on four tenets of energy justice that address the following: (1) distributional - injustices on equitable access to energy services; (2) recognition - ignored and neglected processes to recognize the critical roles of stakeholders in the transition process; (3) procedural - fair process in the transition, acknowledging social and spatial issues; and (4) restorative - negative impacts on people and how they can be fairly compensated..
As a JET approach, Energy Democracy isa key principle, strategy, and process that can ensure decentralization and localization of control and ownership of energy systems, strengthen social accountability thus better distributing the development gains of energy.
In reality, the world&#x2019;s dependence on fossil fuels has come at a huge cost. The industrial growth that has fuelled massive human consumption has led to environmental depletion and catastrophic shifts in climate patterns &#x2013; destructive typhoons, torrential rains, heat waves and droughts &#x2013; that are destroying millions of lives. Poverty levels continues to rise in the Philippines, exacerbated by the global economic recession, the COVID-19 pandemic, democratic turmoil, and many other intersecting issues.
Oxfam Phippines hosted PMU staff (30%).</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="1" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">OXFAM Australia</narrative>
  </participating-org>
  <participating-org ref="GB-CHC-202918" role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam GB Philippines</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam Great Britain#s Philippine office has been in existence since 1987. It has maintained a developmental program in the country that has gradually shifted from relief and basic services provision to a more rights-based developmental approach. Oxfam Great Britain has supported community-based coastal resources management since the early 1990s, as an approach towards an integration of coastal ecosystem rehabilitation, poverty alleviation, and political equity concerns for small-scale sectors.
Oxfam inthe Philippines works towards a future where Filipinos, especially women and other vulnerable sectors, are free from the injustices of poverty. During emergencies, we respond with life-saving aid while supporting people recover their livelihoods. Everyday,we drive change by working hand-in-hand with our partners in finding practical yet innovative ways to help families lift themselves out of poverty by influencing decisions that affect them.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
